    "grammar": [
      [
        "number + classifier + noun",
        "A number cannot go straight onto a noun; a classifier (measure word) comes in between."
      ],
      [
        "tiáo",
        "Classifier for long, winding things: dragons, rivers, snakes."
      ],
      [
        "bǎ",
        "Classifier for things you hold by a handle: swords, fans."
      ],
      [
        "zhī",
        "Classifier for many (mostly smaller) animals: monkeys, birds."
      ],
      [
        "tóu",
        "Classifier for large livestock: pigs, oxen."
      ],
      [
        "bare noun",
        "With no number (after méiyǒu or pà) there is no classifier, and the noun can mean one or many."
      ]
    ],
    "notes": "English does this too with mass nouns (“two loaves of bread”, “three head of cattle”), but Mandarin needs a classifier for every noun. Which one is used is partly about shape and partly just convention; the general-purpose classifier gè is often used in casual speech.",
    "fact": "Hanyu Pinyin, the spelling used here, was officially adopted in China in 1958. Its chief designer, Zhou Youguang, began work on it in his late 40s and lived to the age of 111."
